{%- comment -%}
  Bassface filter drawer — the right-hand panel from the `rmFPV` component.

  A snippet, not a section: it has no settings of its own and no life outside a
  product grid. The collection page renders it today; search results is the next
  caller, which is why every string arrives as a parameter rather than being
  read from `section.settings` in here.

  Accepts:
    filters      - a filter list, i.e. `collection.filters`. Required; render
                   nothing when it is empty (the caller checks, so that the
                   toolbar's Filter trigger disappears with it).
    total        - the current result count, for the "Show Results (12)" button.
                   Pass `paginate.items` — snippets cannot see `paginate`.
    heading      - panel heading. Defaults to "Filters".
    clear_label  - the reset control. Defaults to "Clear All".
    apply_label  - the confirm control. Defaults to "Show Results".
    close_label  - accessible name for the X. Defaults to "Close filters".
    chip_filters - comma-separated filter labels to draw as a chip grid instead
                   of checkbox rows. Defaults to "Size".

  Usage:
    {%- render 'bf-filter-drawer', filters: collection.filters, total: paginate.items -%}

  WHY A SETTING DECIDES CHIPS VS CHECKBOXES. Shopify types both Type and Size as
  `list`; nothing in the data says one is a row of words and the other a grid of
  boxes. The board draws Size as chips because its values are short, so the first
  attempt was to measure the values and switch on length — which turns a design
  decision into a guess that changes when a merchant adds a size called
  "One Size". Naming the filter is explicit, survives new values, and the
  merchant can move it.

  NO PER-OPTION COUNTS, deliberately. Dawn prints "In stock (42)" beside every
  box; the board prints none. That is also what lets this drawer stay in the DOM
  untouched while filters are applied — with counts it would have to re-render
  on every keystroke, and re-rendering a panel the visitor is mid-way through
  moves the checkbox under their cursor.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = heading | default: 'Filters'
  assign clear_label = clear_label | default: 'Clear All'
  assign apply_label = apply_label | default: 'Show Results'
  assign close_label = close_label | default: 'Close filters'
  assign chip_setting = chip_filters | default: 'Size'

  comment
    Downcased and comma-joined so the comparison below is a single `contains`
    against a delimited string — Liquid has no array membership test, and
    `contains` on a bare join would match "Size" inside "One Size Only".
  endcomment
  assign chip_list = chip_setting | downcase | split: ','
  assign chip_keys = ''
  for name in chip_list
    assign trimmed = name | strip
    assign chip_keys = chip_keys | append: '|' | append: trimmed | append: '|'
  endfor
-%}

<bf-filter-drawer class="filter-drawer" id="bf-filter-drawer">
  {%- comment -%}
    The scrim is a sibling of the panel and carries the close hook, so a click
    anywhere off the panel closes. It is not a child of the panel for the
    obvious reason and not on the host element for a less obvious one: the host
    is `position: fixed` with `pointer-events: none` so that a closed drawer
    cannot swallow clicks meant for the grid beneath it.

    THE &nbsp; IS LOAD-BEARING. Dawn's base.css carries a blanket
    `div:empty { display: none }` at (0,1,1), which outranks a bare `.class`
    rule — so this div rendered at 0x0 with its background applied and the page
    behind it simply never dimmed. bf-footer.css and bf-hero.css both hit the
    same rule and record it. Any purely decorative div in this theme needs
    content in it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="filter-drawer__scrim" data-bf-drawer-close>&nbsp;</div>

  <div class="filter-drawer__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="{{ heading }}">
    <div class="filter-drawer__header">
      <h2 class="filter-drawer__heading">{{ heading }}</h2>

      <button class="filter-drawer__close" type="button" data-bf-drawer-close>
        {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'x', size: 24, label: close_label -%}
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      A real GET form to the collection, so the drawer works with the script
      absent: every control is a named input, and the Show Results button
      submits. With the script present the submit is intercepted — see
      bf-filter-drawer.js — and the same field values become a fetched URL.

      `sort_by` is NOT mirrored into a hidden input here. The script carries it
      over from the live URL, and a hidden copy would go stale the moment the
      toolbar's select changed without a reload.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <form class="filter-drawer__body" id="bf-filter-form" method="get" data-filter-form>
      {%- for filter in filters -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A div with role="group", NOT a fieldset with a legend.

          <legend> is laid out against the fieldset's BORDER box, not its
          padding box, so it ignores the group's padding-top entirely: the
          heading sat flush against the divider above it while the options below
          it got the full 32px. There are resets that mostly fix this, all of
          them browser-quirk-dependent. role="group" plus aria-labelledby is the
          same thing to a screen reader and lays out like the box it looks like.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign group_id = 'bf-filter-group-' | append: forloop.index -%}
        <div class="filter-drawer__group" role="group" aria-labelledby="{{ group_id }}">
          <h3 class="filter-drawer__group-title" id="{{ group_id }}">{{ filter.label }}</h3>

          {%- liquid
            assign filter_key = filter.label | downcase | strip
            assign filter_key = '|' | append: filter_key | append: '|'
            assign is_chips = false
            if chip_keys contains filter_key
              assign is_chips = true
            endif
          -%}

          {%- case filter.type -%}
            {%- when 'price_range' -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Two number inputs in the shop's own currency. Dawn's own
                treatment of the values is kept verbatim — `money_without_currency`
                then stripping the thousands separator — because the field posts
                a bare decimal and a value of "2,000" is rejected.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div class="filter-drawer__range">
                {%- comment -%}
                  Decimals are dropped from the bound, which the board also
                  shows bare ("₹2,000", not "₹2,000.00"). A price filter is a
                  coarse control — nobody bounds a search at ₹1,499.00 rather
                  than ₹1,499 — and two trailing zeroes in a 120px field push
                  the digits that matter out of view.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- assign range_max = filter.range_max
                  | money_without_currency
                  | replace: ',', ''
                  | split: '.'
                  | first
                -%}

                <span class="filter-drawer__range-field">
                  <span class="filter-drawer__range-symbol" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{- cart.currency.symbol -}}
                  </span>
                  <label class="visually-hidden" for="bf-filter-price-min">
                    {{ 'products.facets.from' | t }}
                  </label>
                  <input
                    class="filter-drawer__range-input"
                    id="bf-filter-price-min"
                    type="number"
                    inputmode="decimal"
                    min="0"
                    max="{{ range_max }}"
                    placeholder="0"
                    name="{{ filter.min_value.param_name }}"
                    data-filter-price-min
                    {% if filter.min_value.value %}
                      value="{{ filter.min_value.value | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' | split: '.' | first }}"
                    {% endif %}
                  >
                </span>

                <span class="filter-drawer__range-dash" aria-hidden="true">—</span>

                <span class="filter-drawer__range-field">
                  <span class="filter-drawer__range-symbol" aria-hidden="true">
                    {{- cart.currency.symbol -}}
                  </span>
                  <label class="visually-hidden" for="bf-filter-price-max">
                    {{ 'products.facets.to' | t }}
                  </label>
                  <input
                    class="filter-drawer__range-input"
                    id="bf-filter-price-max"
                    type="number"
                    inputmode="decimal"
                    min="0"
                    max="{{ range_max }}"
                    placeholder="{{ range_max }}"
                    name="{{ filter.max_value.param_name }}"
                    data-filter-price-max
                    {% if filter.max_value.value %}
                      value="{{ filter.max_value.value | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' | split: '.' | first }}"
                    {% endif %}
                  >
                </span>
              </div>

              {%- comment -%}
                The slider. Two native <input type="range"> stacked on one
                track, NOT a pair of divs with pointer handlers.

                Native ranges arrive with keyboard support (arrows, Home/End,
                Page Up/Down), a screen-reader role and value, and touch
                behaviour that already works — all of which a div-based thumb
                has to rebuild, usually badly. What they cannot do is show two
                thumbs, so there are two inputs laid over each other with the
                track and fill drawn beneath; bf-filter-drawer.css hands pointer
                events to the thumbs only, so each is grabbable even though the
                inputs overlap completely.

                THEY CARRY NO name= AND SO NEVER REACH THE FORM DATA. The number
                fields above stay the single source of truth for the URL; the
                slider writes into them and they do the rest. Two controls both
                serialising a price is how a slider and a field end up
                disagreeing about what was asked for.

                Spans rather than divs for the track and fill: Dawn's
                `div:empty` rule lists div but not span, and stuffing a &nbsp;
                into a 2px-tall bar to satisfy it would fight the height it is
                supposed to have.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div class="filter-drawer__slider" data-filter-slider>
                <span class="filter-drawer__slider-track"></span>
                <span class="filter-drawer__slider-fill" data-filter-slider-fill></span>

                <input
                  class="filter-drawer__slider-input"
                  type="range"
                  min="0"
                  max="{{ range_max }}"
                  step="1"
                  value="{{ filter.min_value.value | default: 0 | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' | split: '.' | first }}"
                  aria-label="{{ 'products.facets.from' | t }}"
                  data-filter-slider-min
                >

                <input
                  class="filter-drawer__slider-input"
                  type="range"
                  min="0"
                  max="{{ range_max }}"
                  step="1"
                  value="{% if filter.max_value.value %}{{ filter.max_value.value | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' | split: '.' | first }}{% else %}{{ range_max }}{% endif %}"
                  aria-label="{{ 'products.facets.to' | t }}"
                  data-filter-slider-max
                >
              </div>

            {%- else -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                `list` and `boolean` share this branch. Both expose
                `filter.values` with a param_name and an active flag per value,
                and the only difference the board draws is chips vs rows.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div
                class="filter-drawer__options{% if is_chips %} filter-drawer__options--chips{% endif %}"
              >
                {%- for value in filter.values -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    A value matching nothing under the CURRENT filters is drawn
                    but not removed, and stays tickable.

                    Dawn disables these. That is wrong for sizes, which is the
                    case that matters here: "no 2XL in this collection right
                    now" is information a visitor wants, and a size that
                    disappears from the grid the moment an unrelated filter
                    narrows the list reads as a broken control. Marked with a
                    modifier and left operable — ticking it and getting an empty
                    grid is a truthful answer, and Clear All is right there.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <label
                    class="filter-drawer__option{% if is_chips %} filter-drawer__option--chip{% endif %}{% if value.count == 0 %} filter-drawer__option--empty{% endif %}"
                    {% if value.count == 0 %}
                      title="{{ 'products.facets.product_count_simple' | t: count: 0 }}"
                    {% endif %}
                  >
                    {%- comment -%}
                      The real input is visually hidden rather than
                      `appearance: none` — it keeps native checkbox semantics,
                      keyboard behaviour and form serialisation, and the drawn
                      box beside it is what the board paints.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    <input
                      class="filter-drawer__input visually-hidden"
                      type="checkbox"
                      name="{{ value.param_name }}"
                      value="{{ value.value }}"
                      {% if value.active %}
                        checked
                      {% endif %}
                    >

                    {%- unless is_chips -%}
                      <span class="filter-drawer__box" aria-hidden="true">
                        <span class="filter-drawer__tick">
                          {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'check', size: 12 -%}
                        </span>
                      </span>
                    {%- endunless -%}

                    <span class="filter-drawer__option-label">{{ value.label }}</span>
                  </label>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </div>
          {%- endcase -%}
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </form>

    <div class="filter-drawer__footer">
      <button class="filter-drawer__clear" type="button" data-filter-clear>
        {{- clear_label -}}
      </button>

      {%- comment -%}
        Closes the drawer; it does not apply anything. Filters take effect the
        moment they are ticked, because the count on this button has to be a
        real number and the only way to know it is to ask the server — and once
        the server has answered, showing the grid it answered with is free.

        The submit type is the no-JS path: without the script this is the only
        way to send the form, and it does exactly what it says. `form=` rather
        than nesting, because the footer is a flex row pinned below the
        scrolling body and the form IS that scrolling body — the button cannot
        be inside it and stay pinned.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <button class="filter-drawer__apply" type="submit" form="bf-filter-form" data-filter-apply>
        {{ apply_label }} (<span data-filter-total>{{ total }}</span>)
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</bf-filter-drawer>
